    },
    "body": "I found that encryption initializes AES/GCM with supplied keys from the AndroidKeyStore and PBKDF2 paths. The CipherSpec usage rule does not admit the key origin at that call.\n\nI now reject null or non-AES encryption keys, surface unavailable keystore keys as `InvalidKeyException`, and convert the PBKDF2 key material to an AES `SecretKeySpec` for both encryption and recovery. I retain the existing master-key identity and encoded key bytes, leave the AES/GCM setup unchanged, wipe the temporary PBKDF2 byte array, and add coverage for encryption, recovery, and legacy-record compatibility.\n\nI found this while running the app under RVSec, a runtime checker for how apps use the Java crypto and TLS APIs.\n\nI also added a test for it (`EncryptedKeyCipherSpecTest.java`). The project builds with this change (`:mobile:assembleDebugAndroidTest`).\n\nI'm a Computer Engineering student at the University of Brasília (UnB), working on a research project on Android app analysis. Happy to adjust this to the project's conventions.",
